{"name":"Luau Green","hex":"#989746","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"luau","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elaborate Hawaiian feast featuring traditional foods and entertainment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/luau"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lūʻau","blocks":[{"text":"A lūʻau, often anglicized as luau, is a traditional Hawaiian party or feast that is usually accompanied by entertainment. It often features Native Hawaiian cuisine with foods such as poi, kālua puaʻa, poke, lomi salmon, lomi ʻōʻio, ʻopihi, and haupia, and is often accompanied with beer and entertainment such as traditional Hawaiian music, kanikapila, and hula. Among people from Hawaii, the concepts of \"lūʻau\" and \"party\" are often blended, resulting in graduation lūʻau, wedding lūʻau, baby lūʻau, and birthday lūʻau."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Robert_Louis_Stevenson_at_Royal_Luau%2C_1889_%28PP-98-12-004%29_%28mono%29.jpg/330px-Robert_Louis_Stevenson_at_Royal_Luau%2C_1889_%28PP-98-12-004%29_%28mono%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C5%AB%CA%BBau","wikidata":"Q277768"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Food_at_a_traditional_Hawaiian_luau.png/500px-Food_at_a_traditional_Hawaiian_luau.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Food_at_a_traditional_Hawaiian_luau.png/500px-Food_at_a_traditional_Hawaiian_luau.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plate at a wedding lūʻau in Kāneʻohe, Oʻahu in 1996","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFood_at_a_traditional_Hawaiian_luau.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/USMC-11669.jpg/500px-USMC-11669.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/USMC-11669.jpg/500px-USMC-11669.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lūʻau-themed party at Marine Corps Air Station Yuma","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSMC-11669.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Hawaiian